{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 21, :comments ["<b>And Elohim created, etc.</b> Meaning: HaShem added power into the two categories of creation that were brought into being from the waters. And the reason He returned to say ״וְאֵת כָּל נֶפֶשׁ הַחַיָּה״ etc. — and the waters did not suffice to bring them forth, something we do not find regarding the earth's bringing forth the vegetation and herbs, for which the utterance of HaShem sufficed — is this: know that neither the growth-producing agent nor the creator creates more than what exists within the potential of its own offspring, for how can one give what one does not possess? Now, the waters and the dust lack the vital spirit, so whence would they have spirit to implant in their creations? And although the earth and the waters do possess a very subtle vitality, even so, what they give of it they cannot give more than what is within them, and this would not suffice for the fish and the birds. Therefore their power did not suffice, and HaShem needed to create for them a vital spirit, and this is what the verse says: <b>And He created</b> etc. <b>et every nefesh</b> etc. And although in commanding the waters He said ״יִשְׁרְצוּ שֶׁרֶץ נֶפֶשׁ חַיָּה״ — meaning: a swarming creature that would possess the preparedness to receive a living nefesh — for you must know that the vital principle requires a preparatory vessel, and it is intermediary between it and the matter; and this the waters do have the power to produce, as we said, that within their power there is a subtle spiritual force." "And the great taninim — HaShem made a change in their creation, as we have said, and He did not say in a general manner ״וַיִּבְרָא נֶפֶשׁ הַתַּנִּינִים וְאֵת כָּל נֶפֶשׁ״ etc. In this the verse revealed that they are differentiated from everything in the sea, for beyond implanting in them a nefesh, also in the very power of their creation itself HaShem created in them an additional power that the sea has no potency to produce, as we have said. And this is the wondrous fish, the Leviathan; its matters are already explained explicitly in the words of the Sages, may their memory be for a blessing (Bava Batra 74a). And the reason He did not say in this creation ״וַיְהִי כֵן״ is that there is no place for it, for ״וַיְהִי כֵן״ needs to be said only when nothing is mentioned but the utterances of HaShem alone — then the Torah tells us ״וַיְהִי כֵן,״ for He said and it came to be. But when it says ״וַיִּבְרָא״ — this includes saying, and it includes coming-into-being."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T18:56:40.435653380Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}